# The app's own R8 rules (architecture.md §7.2). AGP passes every rule file in src/main/keepRules
# to R8 for staging and release. argon2kt keeps its JNI members with @Keep, and
# kotlinx-serialization ships its own rules, which keep the serializers of every kept
# @Serializable class.
#
# Navigation 3 ships no rules, and its saved back stack finds each route key's serializer by class
# name. So the route keys of nav.Route (the class itself, every class nested in it and every class
# that implements it) keep their names and members; the serialization rules then keep their
# serializers. The staging smoke of every integration check proves it with a back stack restored
# after the process died in the background.
-keep class io.github.dilshodbekmx.passwordvault.nav.Route { *; }
-keep class io.github.dilshodbekmx.passwordvault.nav.Route$** { *; }
-keep class * implements io.github.dilshodbekmx.passwordvault.nav.Route { *; }

# androidx.biometric 1.4.0-alpha07 brings its own FingerprintManager path for Android 6 to 8.1, and
# for the Samsung phones with Android 9 that it routes there (lock.md §9.1). The callback it hands
# to FingerprintManager is the anonymous class below. The android.jar of compileSdk 37 has no
# android.hardware.fingerprint, so R8 cannot see that these four methods override the platform's
# AuthenticationCallback, and the library's own rules only -dontwarn that class: without this rule
# R8 removes all four, and no fingerprint result ever reaches the app on those phones in staging and
# release (the review of build plan 4.1; shown with lock.md E6 on API 24). Build check R-16 fails the
# build of staging and release when one of them is not kept under its own name. The class name is
# the pinned version's: when the version changes, run R-16 and E6 again (lock.md §9.1 item 5).
-keepclassmembers class androidx.biometric.internal.FingerprintManagerCompat$Api23Impl$1 {
    public void onAuthenticationError(int, java.lang.CharSequence);
    public void onAuthenticationHelp(int, java.lang.CharSequence);
    public void onAuthenticationSucceeded(android.hardware.fingerprint.FingerprintManager$AuthenticationResult);
    public void onAuthenticationFailed();
}
